{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01Evt8rhsgk7M1mzAHhPT258","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01KfJzaMmmgs2BhnKqJPcJve","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sarthakagarwal/Desktop/projects/prep/ai-engineering/answers/01-001-how-do-llms-work.md","content":"# How do LLMs work?\n\n**Category:** 01-llm-fundamentals\n**Question #:** 001\n**Source section:** §1 in interview-questions.md\n**Status:** `review`\n**Generated:** ralph-ai-engineering\n\n---\n\n## Framing\n\n### Why this question is asked\nInterviewers use this as an opening calibration question. It separates candidates who have a genuine mechanistic understanding of LLMs from those who have only used APIs. A strong answer reveals whether you understand the training pipeline (next-token prediction at scale), the inference loop (autoregressive decoding), and the architectural pieces (transformer blocks, tokenization, embeddings) — all of which underpin every downstream AI engineering decision.\n\n### Trigger phrases\n- \"Walk me through how a large language model works.\"\n- \"Explain how GPT / Claude generates a response.\"\n- \"At a high level, what is an LLM doing under the hood?\"\n\n### What it tests\nDepth of foundational understanding: can the candidate explain the full loop from raw text → tokens → embeddings → transformer forward pass → next-token prediction → autoregressive decoding — and connect it to practical engineering constraints (cost, latency, context limits)?\n\n---\n\n## Answer\n\n### Concept\nAn LLM is a neural network — specifically a stack of transformer blocks — trained via next-token prediction on hundreds of billions to trillions of tokens of text. At inference time it takes a sequence of tokens as input and outputs a probability distribution over the vocabulary for the next token; sampling from that distribution and appending the result, then repeating, produces coherent text. The emergent ability to reason, follow instructions, and answer questions is a side effect of optimizing this deceptively simple objective at massive scale.\n\n### Mechanism\n**Training:**\n1. **Tokenization** — raw text is split into sub-word tokens using an algorithm like BPE (Byte-Pair Encoding). GPT-4 uses the `cl100k_base` tokenizer with ~100K vocabulary; a typical English word is ~1.3 tokens.\n2. **Embedding** — each token ID maps to a learned dense vector (e.g., 4096 dimensions in LLaMA-3 70B). A positional encoding (sinusoidal or learned RoPE) is added to inject order information, since attention itself is permutation-invariant.\n3. **Transformer forward pass** — the embedded sequence passes through N stacked transformer blocks. Each block runs:\n   - **Multi-head self-attention**: computes Q=xW_Q, K=xW_K, V=xW_V; scores = softmax(Q·Kᵀ/√d_k)·V; runs H attention heads in parallel, concatenates results\n   - **Feed-forward network**: two linear layers with a nonlinearity (GeLU/SwiGLU) — each token position processed independently\n   - **Layer norm + residual connections** stabilize training\n4. **Output projection** — the final hidden state for the last token is projected to logit scores over the full vocabulary via an unembedding matrix (often weight-tied to the embedding matrix).\n5. **Training objective** — cross-entropy loss on the predicted token vs. the actual next token, optimized with AdamW over trillions of steps.\n\n**Inference (autoregressive decoding):**\n- Given a prompt, the model runs a single forward pass to predict the next token.\n- The sampled token is appended, and the model runs again — but with **KV cache**, it only computes attention for the new token against cached keys/values from all prior tokens (O(1) per step instead of O(n²) recomputation).\n- This repeats until a stop token or max-length is reached.\n\n**Post-training alignment (making it useful):**\n- **SFT (Supervised Fine-Tuning)** on instruction-response pairs teaches format.\n- **RLHF or DPO** aligns outputs to human preference — DPO skips the explicit reward model and directly optimizes preference pairs, making it more stable and cheaper than PPO-based RLHF.\n\n### Example / Tradeoff\nA concrete example: GPT-4 has ~1.8T parameters in a Mixture-of-Experts architecture. At inference, only ~2 of 16 expert FFN layers activate per token (MoE routing), so effective active parameters are much lower — trading total model size for inference efficiency. With vLLM's PagedAttention, KV cache memory is managed like virtual memory pages, enabling batching of many concurrent requests without the memory waste of pre-allocating worst-case sequence lengths. At $0.01/1K tokens (GPT-4o pricing), a 10-million-query-per-day app costs ~$100K/month if prompts average 1K tokens — which is exactly why tokenization efficiency and caching matter in production.\n\n**Key constraint:** LLM inference is **memory-bandwidth-bound**, not compute-bound. Loading 70B parameters in FP16 requires 140 GB of memory bandwidth per forward pass step, which is why GPUs with high HBM bandwidth (A100: 2TB/s, H100: 3.35TB/s) matter more than raw FLOP counts for latency.\n\n---\n\n## Verbal script\n\n**Opening (30s):**\n\"Sure — I'll walk through the full loop from raw text to generated output. At the highest level, an LLM is a transformer-based neural network trained to predict the next token in a sequence, and everything — instruction following, reasoning, factual recall — emerges from optimizing that objective at scale. There are three phases worth understanding: how it's trained, how it generates text at inference, and how post-training alignment makes it useful.\"\n\n**Core explanation (2–3 min):**\n\"Starting with training: the model first sees raw text tokenized into sub-word units using BPE — something like 'unhappiness' might become ['un', 'happiness'] or ['unhapin', 'ness'] depending on the vocabulary. Each token is mapped to a learned embedding vector, then positional encodings are added so the model knows token order — since self-attention itself has no notion of position.\n\nThe sequence passes through N transformer blocks. Each block runs multi-head self-attention: for every token, we compute Query, Key, and Value projections, then score each token against every other via dot product divided by √d_k to prevent vanishing softmax gradients. The scores become weights over the value vectors, so each token's output is a weighted blend of all other tokens' values — that's how long-range dependencies are captured. Multiple attention heads run in parallel, each learning different relationship types — one might track subjects and verbs, another track coreference.\n\nAfter attention, each position goes through a feedforward network independently. The final hidden state for the last token is projected to logit scores over the vocabulary — say 100K tokens — and we compute cross-entropy loss against the actual next token. This trains the weights.\n\nAt inference, we do autoregressive decoding: given a prompt, predict the next token, append it, predict again. The critical optimization is KV cache — we store the key and value tensors for every past token so we don't recompute attention from scratch each step. Without KV cache, each new token would require O(n²) attention over the full sequence; with it, it's O(n) amortized.\"\n\n**Tradeoff / production angle (1 min):**\n\"The big engineering constraint is that inference is memory-bandwidth-bound. For a 70B parameter model in FP16, you need ~140 GB just for weights, plus KV cache growing linearly with sequence length and batch size. This is why techniques like quantization (INT8/INT4), model distillation, and KV cache compression matter. In production with vLLM, PagedAttention manages KV cache like virtual memory — avoiding the wasted allocation that would otherwise cap throughput. At scale, the throughput vs. latency tradeoff matters: larger batches = higher throughput but higher time-to-first-token, which hurts interactive UX.\"\n\n**Wrap-up (30s):**\n\"So in short: LLMs are next-token predictors trained at scale on transformers, with autoregressive decoding at inference using KV cache for efficiency, and post-training alignment via SFT + RLHF/DPO to make them useful. Happy to go deeper on any part — the attention math, the training pipeline, or the inference serving stack.\"\n\n---\n\n## Pitfalls\n\n- **Mistake:** Describing LLMs as \"understanding language\" or \"thinking\" without explaining the mechanism — **Better:** Anchor the explanation in the concrete objective: cross-entropy loss on next-token prediction, and that \"understanding\" is an emergent property of scale, not a built-in capability.\n- **Mistake:** Skipping tokenization and going straight to \"it reads your input\" — **Better:** Explicitly mention that input is tokenized first, that token count drives API cost and context limits, and that this affects practical decisions like prompt design and document chunking.\n- **Mistake:** Saying KV cache speeds up training — **Better:** KV cache is an inference-only optimization; training always recomputes the full attention matrix because gradient flow requires it.\n- **Mistake:** Stopping at \"it uses transformers\" without explaining autoregressive decoding — **Better:** Explain the generation loop: one token at a time, sampling from the output distribution, with temperature/top-p controlling the randomness.\n\n---\n\n## Related questions\n\n| Question | Relationship |\n|----------|--------------|\n| [Q2: How do transformers work?](01-002-how-do-transformers-work.md) | Deep dive into the architecture described here at a mechanistic level |\n| [Q9: What is KV cache? How does it help in LLM inference?](01-009-what-is-kv-cache-how-does-it-help-in-llm-inference.md) | Expands on the inference optimization mentioned in the mechanism section |\n| [Q4: What is the difference between pre-training and fine-tuning?](01-004-what-is-the-difference-between-pre-training-and-fine-tuning.md) | Covers the post-training alignment phase referenced in the answer |\n\n---\n\n## One-liner recall\n\n> LLMs are transformer stacks trained via next-token prediction at massive scale, generating text autoregressively one token at a time using KV-cached attention, then aligned to instructions via SFT + RLHF/DPO — with inference bottlenecked by memory bandwidth, not compute.\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5485,"cache_read_input_tokens":50399,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5485,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f3b2d6e-f3f0-4269-97ea-7056b52ecd18","uuid":"e2dfbefb-8ed5-4687-9752-83aa6ec108c1"}
