{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01YMWPM4mvmfgtY8QbR32r69","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_011gPhnzEahx6MD5zt7WaRfP","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sarthakagarwal/Desktop/projects/prep/ai-engineering/answers/01-002-how-do-transformers-work.md","content":"# How do transformers work?\n\n**Category:** 01-llm-fundamentals\n**Question #:** 002\n**Source section:** §1 in interview-questions.md\n**Status:** `review`\n**Generated:** ralph-ai-engineering\n\n---\n\n## Framing\n\n### Why this question is asked\nThis is the single most common technical screen question for AI engineering roles — asked at every level from L3 to staff. Interviewers want to see that you understand the mechanism that makes modern LLMs possible: the self-attention operation. Weak candidates describe transformers as \"better RNNs\" or recite buzzwords. Strong candidates walk through the Q/K/V computation, explain why it works better than recurrence, and connect it to practical tradeoffs (quadratic cost, KV cache, positional encoding choices).\n\n### Trigger phrases\n- \"How do transformers work?\"\n- \"Explain the transformer architecture.\"\n- \"What is self-attention and why is it important?\"\n- \"Why did transformers replace RNNs/LSTMs?\"\n- \"Walk me through a transformer forward pass.\"\n\n### What it tests\nMechanistic understanding of the core neural architecture underpinning all modern LLMs — specifically: whether the candidate can explain self-attention mathematically, describe the full transformer block, and articulate why this design scales better than predecessors.\n\n---\n\n## Answer\n\n### Concept\nThe transformer is a neural network architecture built on self-attention: a mechanism that computes, for every token in a sequence, a weighted combination of all other tokens' representations — allowing direct, long-range information flow regardless of sequence length. Unlike RNNs, which process tokens sequentially and compress history into a fixed state vector (losing distant information), transformers process the entire sequence in parallel, making them both faster to train and better at capturing long-range dependencies.\n\n### Mechanism\nA transformer is a stack of identical blocks. Each block has two core sub-layers, with residual connections and layer normalization wrapping each:\n\n**1. Multi-Head Self-Attention**\n\nGiven an input sequence of embeddings X ∈ ℝ^(n×d_model), self-attention computes:\n\n- **Q = X·W_Q** (queries), **K = X·W_K** (keys), **V = X·W_V** (values) — each projected to dimension d_k\n- **Attention scores:** A = softmax(Q·Kᵀ / √d_k) — the √d_k scaling prevents vanishing gradients when d_k is large\n- **Output:** Attention(Q, K, V) = A·V — a weighted sum of values, where high-scoring token pairs get more weight\n\nMulti-head attention runs H parallel attention operations with different weight matrices (W_Q^i, W_K^i, W_V^i), then concatenates their outputs and projects back to d_model. This lets each head learn different relationship types — one head might track syntactic dependencies, another coreference.\n\n**2. Position-wise Feed-Forward Network (FFN)**\n\nAfter attention, each token position is independently transformed by:\n- FFN(x) = ReLU(x·W_1 + b_1)·W_2 + b_2 (or with SwiGLU/GELU activation in modern models)\n- Typical expansion factor: 4× the model dimension (d_ff = 4·d_model)\n\n**3. Positional Encoding**\n\nSelf-attention is permutation-invariant — it sees no order. Positional encodings inject order information. Original transformers used sinusoidal functions; modern LLMs use RoPE (Rotary Position Embedding), which encodes relative positions directly into Q and K projections and generalizes better to unseen sequence lengths.\n\n**4. Layer Norm + Residual Connections**\n\nEach sub-layer wraps its computation as:\n- output = LayerNorm(x + SubLayer(x))\n\nThis allows very deep stacks (GPT-3: 96 layers; Llama-3-405B: 126 layers) without vanishing gradients.\n\n**5. Decoder vs Encoder difference**\n\n- **Encoder-only (BERT):** Uses bidirectional attention — each token attends to all other tokens. Used for classification, embeddings, NER.\n- **Decoder-only (GPT, Llama, Claude):** Uses causal (masked) self-attention — each token can only attend to itself and prior tokens. Enables autoregressive text generation.\n- **Encoder-decoder (T5, BART):** Encoder processes input with full attention; decoder attends to its own past tokens (causal) plus cross-attends to encoder output.\n\n**6. Full forward pass (decoder-only, inference)**\n1. Tokenize input → token IDs\n2. Look up token embeddings + add positional encoding (RoPE)\n3. Pass through N transformer blocks (attention + FFN + residuals)\n4. Final hidden state → linear projection to vocab size → softmax → probability distribution\n5. Sample next token (greedy / top-p / temperature); append to sequence; repeat\n\n### Example / Tradeoff\n**Concrete numbers:** GPT-3 (175B params) has 96 layers, 12,288 d_model, 96 heads (d_k = 128 per head), d_ff = 49,152. A forward pass on a 4K-token sequence requires ~350 GFLOPs. Training required ~3.14×10²³ FLOPs total — at A100 throughput (~312 TFLOPS), that's ~11,000 GPU-days.\n\n**The key tradeoff:** Self-attention is O(n²) in sequence length — a 128K-token context costs 1024× more attention compute than a 4K context. This is why:\n- FlashAttention rewrites the attention kernel to be IO-aware, reducing memory from O(n²) to O(n) by keeping attention matrices in SRAM rather than HBM\n- Sparse/sliding-window attention (Longformer, Mistral) approximates full attention for very long sequences\n- KV cache stores past tokens' K and V projections, so decoding each new token is O(n) total (amortized O(1) per step) rather than recomputing all prior tokens' attention\n\n---\n\n## Verbal script\n\n**Opening (30s):**\n\"The transformer is the architecture that enabled the LLM revolution. I'll walk through why it was a breakthrough over RNNs, the core self-attention mechanism, the full block structure, and the practical tradeoffs that matter in production.\"\n\n**Core explanation (2–3 min):**\n\"Before transformers, RNNs and LSTMs processed sequences token-by-token, compressing all prior context into a fixed-size hidden state. That created a bottleneck — long-range dependencies got lost, and sequential processing couldn't be parallelized across GPUs.\n\nTransformers solve both problems with self-attention. The idea: for every token, compute how much it should attend to every other token, then take a weighted blend of all their value representations.\n\nMechanically: we project the input embeddings into three spaces — Queries, Keys, and Values — using learned weight matrices. We compute similarity between queries and keys via dot product, scaled by √d_k to prevent the softmax from saturating, then apply softmax to get attention weights. These weights score the value vectors, and we sum them. That gives us, for each token, a new representation that's informed by the entire context.\n\nMulti-head attention runs this H times in parallel with different projections — each head can learn different relationship types. You concatenate the heads and project back to model dimension.\n\nAfter attention, a position-wise feed-forward network applies a non-linear transformation to each token independently — this is where a lot of the 'factual knowledge' is believed to be stored. Each block wraps both sub-layers with residual connections and layer norm to enable training 96+ layer deep stacks.\n\nSince attention is order-agnostic, we add positional encodings — modern models use RoPE, which encodes relative position into the query and key projections directly, and generalizes to longer sequences than the training length.\"\n\n**Tradeoff / production angle (1 min):**\n\"The main cost of transformers is the O(n²) attention computation — attending every token to every other token. For a 128K context, this is 1024× more expensive than a 4K context. FlashAttention solves the memory part by being IO-aware — it keeps attention matrices in SRAM rather than writing to HBM, trading arithmetic ops for memory bandwidth. But it doesn't reduce the FLOP count.\n\nIn production, long contexts also explode KV cache size — storing K and V tensors for 128K tokens at 70B model scale can be 20–40GB per request. This is why GQA (Grouped Query Attention) is used in Llama-3 and Mistral — multiple query heads share a single key-value head, reducing KV cache memory by 4–8×.\"\n\n**Wrap-up (30s):**\n\"So the core insight: self-attention replaced recurrence by letting every token directly attend to every other token in parallel — enabling long-range context and massive GPU parallelism during training. The O(n²) cost is the main constraint for long contexts in production, addressed by FlashAttention, GQA, and KV cache management. Happy to go deeper on any piece — the positional encoding math, GQA, or the training dynamics.\"\n\n---\n\n## Pitfalls\n\n- **Mistake:** Saying \"attention helps the model focus on important words\" without explaining Q·Kᵀ/√d_k and softmax weighting — **Better:** Walk through the matrix operations: Q and K compute pairwise similarity via dot product, scaled to prevent softmax saturation, then used as weights on the V matrix — this is mechanistically distinct from \"paying attention\" in the intuitive sense.\n- **Mistake:** Saying attention is O(n) in sequence length — **Better:** Self-attention is O(n²) in both time and memory (n tokens × n attention scores). This is the fundamental scalability constraint for long-context models, and explaining how FlashAttention, sparse attention, or KV cache address it shows production awareness.\n- **Mistake:** Confusing encoder and decoder transformers — treating \"transformer\" as synonymous with bidirectional/BERT-style — **Better:** Distinguish encoder-only (bidirectional, BERT), decoder-only (causal, GPT/Llama/Claude), and encoder-decoder (T5/BART) and explain when each is appropriate.\n- **Mistake:** Not mentioning why transformers replaced RNNs — **Better:** The key advantages are parallelism during training (all tokens processed simultaneously, not sequentially) and direct long-range attention (no bottleneck hidden state that degrades over distance).\n\n---\n\n## Related questions\n\n| Question | Relationship |\n|----------|--------------|\n| [Q1: How do LLMs work?](01-001-how-do-llms-work.md) | Prerequisite high-level context — transformers are the architecture inside LLMs |\n| [Q22: What is self-attention? How does it differ from multi-head attention?](01-022-what-is-self-attention-how-does-it-differ-from-multi-head-at.md) | Deep dive on the attention sub-layer described here |\n| [Q9: What is KV cache? How does it help in LLM inference?](01-009-what-is-kv-cache-how-does-it-help-in-llm-inference.md) | Follows from the inference/memory tradeoff discussed in mechanism section |\n\n---\n\n## One-liner recall\n\n> Transformers stack self-attention (Q·Kᵀ/√d_k → softmax → weighted V) and position-wise FFN blocks with residuals — enabling parallel, long-range context modeling that scales to billions of parameters, at the cost of O(n²) attention complexity.\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":216,"cache_read_input_tokens":64598,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":216,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f3b2d6e-f3f0-4269-97ea-7056b52ecd18","uuid":"ed1d6073-0fac-4255-a30c-7d81b99bd42c"}
