{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01LYPgYGBYCcFfyPHrEJWdux","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01XA3YNyLLew4h51bmSwd7MN","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sarthakagarwal/Desktop/projects/prep/ai-engineering/answers/01-003-what-is-tokenization-and-how-does-it-affect-llm-performance.md","content":"# What is tokenization and how does it affect LLM performance?\n\n**Category:** 01-llm-fundamentals\n**Question #:** 003\n**Source section:** §1 in interview-questions.md\n**Status:** `review`\n**Generated:** ralph-ai-engineering\n\n---\n\n## Framing\n\n### Why this question is asked\nTokenization is the first step in every LLM pipeline, and it has concrete, measurable effects on cost, context window consumption, and output quality. Interviewers ask this to separate engineers who treat LLMs as black boxes from those who understand the full input pipeline. It's also a gateway to cost/latency discussions: if you don't understand tokenization, you can't reason about API billing, prompt optimization, or why domain-specific text tokenizes poorly with general-purpose vocabularies.\n\n### Trigger phrases\n- \"What is tokenization and why does it matter?\"\n- \"How does tokenization affect context window usage?\"\n- \"Why does the same text cost different amounts with different models?\"\n- \"What is BPE? How does GPT tokenize text?\"\n- \"Why does code / medical text use more tokens than you'd expect?\"\n\n### What it tests\nUnderstanding of the full LLM input pipeline — specifically: how text is converted to token IDs, what algorithm governs vocabulary construction (BPE), and how tokenization choices cascade into cost, latency, and context limits.\n\n---\n\n## Answer\n\n### Concept\nTokenization is the process of splitting raw text into discrete units called tokens before it enters the model. Tokens are sub-word units — not characters, not words — that balance vocabulary coverage against sequence efficiency. The tokenizer converts text to a sequence of integer IDs (the vocabulary), which are then embedded into vectors. Every LLM has a fixed tokenizer (e.g., GPT-4 uses `cl100k_base`, Llama-3 uses its own tiktoken-style BPE), and token count is the unit of billing, context window consumption, and inference cost.\n\n### Mechanism\n**Byte-Pair Encoding (BPE) — the dominant algorithm:**\n1. Start with character-level vocabulary (all bytes or Unicode chars)\n2. Iteratively merge the most frequent adjacent pair of tokens into a new token\n3. Repeat until vocabulary reaches target size (typically 32K–128K tokens)\n4. The result: common words become single tokens; rare words split into sub-tokens\n\nExample with `cl100k_base` (GPT-4):\n- \"tokenization\" → [\"token\", \"ization\"] (2 tokens)\n- \"Llama\" → [\"Ll\", \"ama\"] (2 tokens, because \"Ll\" is a frequent pair in the training corpus)\n- `print(\"hello\")` → [\"print\", '(\"', \"hello\", '\")'] (4 tokens — code tokenizes compactly)\n- \"supercalifragilistic\" → [\"super\", \"cal\", \"if\", \"rag\", \"il\", \"istic\"] (6 tokens — rare word fragments)\n\n**Key tokenization facts:**\n- Average English text: ~0.75 words/token (or ~4 chars/token)\n- GPT-4 (`cl100k_base`): ~100K vocab. Llama-3: ~128K vocab. Older GPT-3 (`p50k_base`): 50K vocab.\n- Token count = input tokens + output tokens = what you pay for and what fills the context window\n- Whitespace handling matters: \"token\" and \" token\" (leading space) are often different token IDs\n\n**Performance effects:**\n\n| Effect | How tokenization causes it |\n|--------|---------------------------|\n| **API cost** | Input tokens + output tokens × price/token. A 10K-word document ≈ 13K tokens ≈ $0.13 at GPT-4o pricing ($0.01/1K tokens). Prompt compression = fewer tokens = direct cost reduction. |\n| **Context window** | A 128K-token context window holds ≈96K English words. But dense code or repetitive structured data can consume far more tokens per \"word\" than expected. |\n| **Latency** | More tokens in = more KV cache memory needed = potentially slower decoding. More tokens out = more autoregressive steps = linearly more latency. |\n| **Quality** | Domain-specific vocabulary (medical, legal, code) tokenizes inefficiently with general-purpose tokenizers — a medical term like \"hyponatremia\" may split into 4–6 subword tokens, making it harder for the model to \"see\" the term as a unit. |\n| **Arithmetic** | LLMs notoriously fail at arithmetic partly because numbers tokenize inconsistently: \"12345\" might be [\"1\", \"23\", \"45\"] while \"12346\" is [\"123\", \"46\"] — making character-level number comparisons opaque to the model. |\n\n### Example / Tradeoff\nA concrete production scenario: a legal contract platform feeds 50-page PDFs to GPT-4. Each page ≈ 500 words ≈ 650 tokens. A 50-page document = 32,500 tokens. At $0.01/1K input tokens, each document costs $0.325 to process — before retrieval. With 300K contracts processed monthly, that's $97,500/month in tokenization costs alone. This is why RAG (retrieve only relevant chunks) dramatically reduces cost vs. full-document stuffing.\n\n**WordPiece vs BPE:** WordPiece (BERT) optimizes for likelihood of the training data rather than raw merge frequency; produces similar results but slightly different splits for rare words. Character-level tokenizers (rare in LLMs) produce very long sequences — a 100-word sentence becomes ~500 characters — making attention quadratically expensive. Subword tokenization is the practical sweet spot.\n\n---\n\n## Verbal script\n\n**Opening (30s):**\n\"Tokenization is the first thing that happens to any input, and it has direct consequences for cost, context window capacity, and output quality. I'll explain what it is, how BPE works, and then give concrete examples of how it affects production systems.\"\n\n**Core explanation (2–3 min):**\n\"Tokenization converts raw text into a sequence of integer token IDs that the model can process. The key insight is that tokens are sub-word units — not characters, not whole words. The dominant algorithm is Byte-Pair Encoding, or BPE.\n\nBPE builds its vocabulary iteratively: it starts at the character level and keeps merging the most frequent adjacent pair of characters or sub-tokens into a new combined token. You do this until you hit your vocabulary size limit — typically 32K to 128K tokens. The result is a vocabulary where common words like 'the', 'is', 'and' are single tokens, while rare words like 'hyponatremia' split into 4–6 sub-tokens.\n\nGPT-4 uses the cl100k_base tokenizer with about 100K vocabulary tokens. On average, English text runs about 0.75 words per token — so a 1,000-word essay is roughly 1,300 tokens. That number is what determines your API bill, your context window consumption, and your inference latency.\n\nThe performance effects cascade from there: more input tokens means more memory for KV cache, which slows decoding. More output tokens means more autoregressive steps — linearly more latency. And poorly tokenized domain text — like medical terminology or long chemical names — can fragment into many sub-tokens, making it harder for the model to reason about those terms as semantic units.\"\n\n**Tradeoff / production angle (1 min):**\n\"The main production tradeoff is cost vs. context coverage. If you stuff a full 50-page legal document into context, you might use 30K tokens at $0.30 per document. Multiply by a million documents and that's $300K in tokenization costs before any model inference. This is exactly why RAG exists — retrieve only the relevant 500 tokens instead of the full 30K.\n\nThe other tradeoff is general vs. domain-specific vocabularies. Code, medical text, and structured JSON all tokenize differently from English prose. Code can actually tokenize quite efficiently because identifiers and keywords appear frequently in the training corpus. But specialized medical or legal Latin terms may split into character-level noise, degrading model performance on those inputs — which is one argument for domain-specific fine-tuning or domain-adapted tokenizers.\"\n\n**Wrap-up (30s):**\n\"So the key point: tokenization converts text to integer sub-word IDs via BPE; token count drives API cost, context limits, and latency; and domain-specific text tokenizes less efficiently than general English. Understanding your tokenizer is the first step to optimizing any production LLM pipeline.\"\n\n---\n\n## Pitfalls\n\n- **Mistake:** Saying \"LLMs process words\" or \"split by spaces\" — **Better:** LLMs process tokens (sub-word units from BPE), not words. \"Tokenization\" itself might split into two tokens. Whitespace is part of the token in many tokenizers (GPT-4's tokenizer treats \" hello\" and \"hello\" as different token IDs).\n- **Mistake:** Not connecting tokenization to cost — treating it as a purely technical detail — **Better:** Token count = billing unit. At production scale, understanding token efficiency directly determines cost. Prompt compression, caching, and retrieval strategies all exist to reduce token count.\n- **Mistake:** Claiming all text tokenizes at ~0.75 words/token regardless of domain — **Better:** English prose averages ~0.75 words/token, but code can be more efficient (~0.3–0.5 words/token for Python), while dense unicode, rare medical terms, or languages with long words (German compound nouns) tokenize much less efficiently — sometimes 1–2 characters per token.\n\n---\n\n## Related questions\n\n| Question | Relationship |\n|----------|--------------|\n| [Q39: What is tokenization, and why does it matter for cost and context windows?](01-039-what-is-tokenization-and-why-does-it-matter-for-cost-and-con.md) | Covers the same topic with a cost/context window focus — this Q is the foundational version |\n| [Q24: BPE vs WordPiece vs character-level tokenization — tradeoffs?](01-024-bpe-vs-wordpiece-vs-character-level-tokenization-tradeoffs.md) | Deep dive on tokenization algorithm variants mentioned in the mechanism section |\n| [Q37: Risks of general-purpose tokenizers on legal/medical domains?](01-037-risks-of-general-purpose-tokenizers-on-legalmedical-domains.md) | Expands on the domain-specific tokenization problem raised in Example/Tradeoff |\n\n---\n\n## One-liner recall\n\n> Tokenization (BPE) splits text into sub-word integer IDs averaging ~0.75 words/token for English — token count directly sets API cost, context window consumption, and inference latency, with domain-specific text (medical, legal) tokenizing less efficiently than general prose.\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2728,"cache_read_input_tokens":69119,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2728,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3f3b2d6e-f3f0-4269-97ea-7056b52ecd18","uuid":"220a9f80-eaf8-4675-a751-233ae38155f8"}
